<DocsLayout title="Notes & Reference" description="Free-form notes, reference notes, spreadsheet-style reference tables and reference images in Ravenburst.">
  <h1>Notes &amp; Reference</h1>
  <p class="lede">Somewhere for everything that isn't the book: ideas, research, timelines, maps. Nothing here is ever compiled.</p>

  <h2 id="notes">Notes</h2>
  <p>The <strong>+</strong> next to Notes adds a note: an ordinary document for brainstorming, to-do lists, cut scenes or anything else. Notes have the full editor, including comments and resume notes.</p>

  <h2 id="reference">Reference</h2>
  <p>The <strong>+</strong> next to Reference offers three kinds of item.</p>

  <h3>Text Note</h3>
  <p>A document for research: sources, quotes, facts to check. In a reference note, the table button on the Format Bar inserts a spreadsheet-style table with formulas and a total row.</p>

  <h3 id="tables">Table</h3>
  <p>A reference table is a sheet of one or more tables, made for timelines, family trees, word lists and continuity notes.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Add Table</strong> adds another table to the same sheet; each has its own title. A table's delete button asks before removing it.</li>
    <li>Each table has its own header and row-stripe colours.</li>
    <li>Right-click a cell for Cut, Copy and Paste; <strong>Set as Number</strong> or <strong>Set as Text</strong>; background and text colour; a border on any side; Clear Cell Style; and Clear Cell.</li>
    <li>Number columns can have a <strong>Σ</strong> total row, and the formula builder adds calculations.</li>
    <li>Move between cells with the arrow keys.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="images">Image</h3>
  <p>A picture to look at while you write: a map, a portrait, a photo of a place. Scroll to zoom (50% to 400%), drag to move around, and double-click or press <strong>Fit</strong> to see the whole image again. Each image has a caption.</p>

  <h2 id="tips">Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Open a note or reference item in <a href="/docs/views#split-view">Split View</a> to keep it beside your writing.</li>
    <li><span class="menu">Documents → Move To</span> moves a scene into Notes (or a note into the Manuscript) without copying and pasting.</li>
    <li><span class="menu">Edit → Find → Find in Project</span> searches notes and reference along with the manuscript.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
